AB  - Critical thinking is now widely recognized as a major educational goal. Among the emerging responses to this challenge are teaching formats that seek to combine rigor, engagement, and compatibility with school constraints. This article examines a lecture-performance, The Critical Moment, designed for school audiences aged 15 to 18, as a bimodal pedagogical device situated at the intersection of lecture and stage performance. The article offers a design-based analysis of the conception of the device. It reconstructs its pedagogical architecture, documents its iterative development, and makes explicit the main design conjectures as well as the pedagogical affordances the format is hypothesised to support. The overall intervention combines a stage performance, a mediation phase, and a written pedagogical resource. It targets a limited set of epistemic objects, including intuitive reasoning, cognitive biases, the distinction between correlation and causation, standards of evidence, pseudoscientific rhetoric, and the burden of proof. The analysis examines how the lecture-performance was designed to support conceptual accessibility through embodied narration, to offer conditions for metacognitive awareness through the staging of cognitive traps, and to engage attention, memorisation, and critical distancing through humour, affect, and guided discussion. The article argues that its transferable contribution lies in the explicitation of this pedagogical design logic, which may inform future research and the design of educational interventions in the teaching of critical thinking.
