AB  - Neurodivergent students experience higher education through an interconnected ecology of teaching, assessment, support services, communication channels, and digital platforms. Yet the presence of accommodations or technologies says little about whether students can find, trust, use, and benefit from them. This systematic review examines how cognitive, behavioral, social, technological, and institutional conditions shape the pathway from support availability to student success. Guided by the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses, the review synthesized 163 Scopus-indexed journal publications from 2021 to 2026 using focused bibliometric profiling, structured thematic synthesis, evidence mapping, and a four-criterion diagnostic readiness assessment. Evidence was concentrated in institutional accommodation and support services (46 publications), student experience and wellbeing (36), digital and emerging mechanisms (29), academic performance and learning regulation (27), and retention and transition pathways (25). Within the digital and emerging domain, 20 publications directly examined technology-mediated interaction, but most focused on access or immediate experience rather than sustained use, governance, or longitudinal outcomes. The synthesis identifies support-to-success decoupling as the central problem: provision may exist without becoming accessible interaction, trusted use, coordinated delivery, measurable benefit, and institutional learning. The review contributes a nested evidence model and a seven-layer readiness framework linking accessibility, cognitive fit, disclosure safety, service integration, digital and artificial intelligence governance, and lifecycle outcome monitoring. Neuroinclusive higher education should therefore be judged by the quality of interaction and the outcomes it enables, not by the mere presence of services or technologies.
