AB  - The advent of generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) is significantly altering both the content taught in business schools and how faculty design and deliver their courses, assess student learning, and develop their teaching skills. This article addresses a critical question for faculty: How can educators integrate GenAI into their teaching practices without becoming overwhelmed by the constant influx of new technological applications? This article distils a “minutes, not months” playbook that translates large language model (LLM) power into four high-impact faculty workflows: (1) curricular design and instructional content preparation; (2) teaching delivery; (3) assessment, feedback, and student support; and (4) evaluating teaching effectiveness and faculty development. Throughout each, we pair workflows with evidence of learning outcome parities (or gains), turnkey toolkits, example prompts, and ethical guardrails. The result is a practical guide that shifts a faculty orientation from a more traditional content producer to a strategic learning architect, enabling business school faculty to meet escalating demands for relevance, personalization, and lifelong learning in an AI-accelerated landscape.
