AB  - Maximizing, the tendency to strive for the best possible option, has often been associated with lower satisfaction. However, findings are mixed, suggesting that its positive associations may depend on the stakes of the decision and on how maximizing is measured. In the context of undergraduate education, we examined whether maximizing is associated with academic performance and academic satisfaction, and whether these associations vary across students at different academic grade levels. Two survey studies were conducted at a four-year university in the United States. Study 1 assessed multidimensional maximizing (high standards, alternative search, decision difficulty), whereas Study 2 used a unidimensional measure emphasizing high standards. Across both studies, maximizing, especially its high-standards component in the multidimensional measure, was positively associated with higher self-reported grade point average and greater academic satisfaction. Grade-year differences were partial and outcome-specific and should not be interpreted as evidence of within-person developmental change. Overall, the findings suggest that maximizing may be positively associated with academic outcomes when it reflects the pursuit of high standards.
